    <DataNode elementId="Type-1-H-Antigen" textLabel="an H type 1 histo-blood group antigen" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="MNXM163303" dataSource="MetaNetX" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="310" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, zhang, SRI, caspi">The |FRAME: H-antigen &quot;H antigens&quot;| are fucosylated oligosaccharides that decorate proteins on the surface of red blood cells in people with O blood type. In people with A or B blood types an additional monosaccharide is attached to one of the &amp;beta;-D-galactosyl residues of the H antigen (|FRAME: CPD-12557| in |FRAME: A-antigen &quot;A antigens&quot;| and |FRAME: GALACTOSE| in |FRAME: B-antigen &quot;B antigens&quot;|). These fucosylated structures are found not only on blood cells but also on many other cell types (mainly epithehal), and it has been suggested that they should be called histo-blood group antigens. The majority of the A, B and H antigens are linked to the ends of long polylactosamine chains attached mainly to band 3 protein (encoded by SLC4A1), the anion exchange protein of the red blood cells. A minor fraction of the antigens is attached to a ceramide moiety, forming neutral glycosphingolipids. Several H antigens exist, differing in their core disaccharide |CITS: [2093726]|. The different types are: |FRAME: Type-1-H-Antigen &quot;Type 1&quot;|: &amp;alpha;Fuc-(1,2)-&amp;beta;Gal-(1&amp;rarr;3)-&amp;beta;GlcNac-(1&amp;rarr;R) |FRAME: Type-2-H-Antigen &quot;Type 2&quot;|: &amp;alpha;Fuc-(1,2)-&amp;beta;Gal-(1&amp;rarr;4)-&amp;beta;GlcNac-(1&amp;rarr;R) |FRAME: Type-3-H-Antigen &quot;Type 3&quot;|: &amp;alpha;Fuc-(1,2)-&amp;beta;Gal-(1&amp;rarr;3)-&amp;alpha;GalNac-(1&amp;rarr;R) |FRAME: Type-4-H-Antigen &quot;Type 4&quot;|: &amp;alpha;Fuc-(1,2)-&amp;beta;Gal-(1&amp;rarr;3)-&amp;beta;GalNac-(1&amp;rarr;R) |FRAME: Type-5-H-Antigen &quot;Type 5&quot;|: &amp;alpha;Fuc-(1,2)-&amp;beta;Gal-(1&amp;rarr;3)-&amp;beta;Gal-(1&amp;rarr;R) |FRAME: Type-6-H-Antigen &quot;Type 6&quot;|: &amp;alpha;Fuc-(1,2)-&amp;beta;Gal-(1&amp;rarr;4)-&amp;beta;Glc-(1&amp;rarr;R) Most of the H antigens found in the cell are either type 1 or type 2, followed by type 3 and 4. Type 1 is found in secretions and in the serum, while type 2 is found exclusively on the surface of red blood cells. Type 6 molecules have been described in exocrine secretions. It is not clear if type 5 H antigens exist, although the type 5 precursor has been characterized |CITS: [2093726]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="Type-1-H-Antigen" />
